window.docContents[194].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Weighted-moment-bridge":{"contents":"For a scalar field , measure , and spatial weight , defineThe spatial type and measure are arbitrary. Euclidean geometry enters only\nthrough a model-specific choice of weight. Mathlib's Bochner integral is\ntotalized, so the weighted spatial moment is defined even for a nonintegrable\nintegrand, where the integral is set to zero. Every theorem that reads this\nexpression as a moment therefore assumes integrability.\n\nAgainst a nonnegative weight, the moment cannot exceed the largest value of\nthe field times the mass of the weight.\n\nIf  is almost everywhere nonnegative and integrable, and\n is integrable, thenBecause  takes values in the extended nonnegative reals, this estimate\nalso covers unbounded slices without an auxiliary boundedness hypothesis. The\nweight need not have mass one.\n\nFirst bound the absolute value of the Bochner integral by the integral of the\nabsolute value and rewrite that integral as a nonnegative extended integral.\nAlmost everywhere, nonnegativity of  and the definition of  give\n\nMonotonicity of the nonnegative Lebesgue integral permits this pointwise bound, after which the\nconstant  is pulled outside. Integrability and nonnegativity of \nidentify the remaining extended integral with\n.\n\nDividing the estimate by a positive mass turns it into a lower bound for\n in terms of the moment, so once a PDE argument has driven\n to infinity the supremum must follow.\n\nIf the nonnegative integrable weight has positive mass, the weighted field is\nintegrable eventually, and  as\n, then the extended pointwise spatial supremum tends to\n.The theorem works along any filter of times and needs no spatial slice\nbound. A PDE-specific theorem supplies divergence of the chosen moment;\neventual finiteness of the slices is the additional condition needed to\nconclude divergence of the spatial supremum norm.\n\nDivide the weighted-moment estimate by the positive finite mass\n. The resulting quantity\n tends to  and is\neventually bounded above by . The order characterization of\nconvergence to the top element therefore proves divergence of the extended\nspatial supremum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Finite-Time Singularity Interfaces","header":"6.7.1.5.3. Weighted-moment bridge","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Weighted-moment-bridge"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Weak-Compactness-for-the-Direct-Method":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.4. Weak Compactness for the Direct Method","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Weak-Compactness-for-the-Direct-Method"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Symbolic-Calculus-and-Analytic-Bridges":{"contents":"\n\nFor a scalar field , the coordinate derivative is\n: evaluate its Fréchet derivative on the\nth basis vector. A symbolic derivative must recover this same linear\napproximation. The partial-derivative bridge proves that it does, under the domain\nconditions of the expression being differentiated. For example,\n is valid whenever\n; neither coordinate has to be nonzero on its own.\n\nAt every point satisfying the generated validity conditions, symbolic\ndifferentiation in coordinate  agrees with our analytic Euclidean\npartial derivative:\n.\n\nStructural induction on the expression constructs its Fréchet derivative\nusing the analytic sum, product, quotient, power, and elementary chain rules.\nThe validity hypotheses discharge each rule's domain conditions. Evaluating\nthe resulting continuous linear map on  gives exactly the evaluated\nsymbolic derivative, hence the stated coordinate identity.\n\nThe validity predicate that justifies one symbolic derivative also certifies\nregularity of every order. At a point where ValidAt holds, evaluation of a\nreflected expression is  for every smoothness order , the analytic\norder included, because each constructor is interpreted by an elementary\nfunction that Mathlib proves smooth of every order away from its\nsingularities; the On and global forms assume validity at every point of\nthe set or the space. The theorems are stated for the two conventions of the\ntheorem-facing tactics, functions on  and functions on\n with frozen parameters, and they are what cas applies\nto a DifferentiableAt, Differentiable, ContDiffAt, ContDiffOn, or\nContDiff goal:\n\nStructural induction on the expression. Constants and coordinate projections\nare smooth; sums, products, and negations preserve ContDiffAt; quotients,\nlogarithms, square roots, real powers, and the inverse sine and cosine are\nsmooth at every point satisfying the validity clause of their constructor, by\nMathlib's ContDiffAt lemmas for the elementary functions and the field\noperations; the remaining elementary functions are smooth everywhere. The derivative bridge also gives differentiability, and the set and global\nforms quantify these pointwise statements over their domain.\n\nAt a point where the scalar inputs being differentiated are valid, the\ncoordinatewise bridge gives gradient, divergence, and three-dimensional curl\nformulas. The Laplacian bridge additionally assumes validity of every first\nsymbolic partial at that point. Hessians, Jacobians, symmetric\ngradients, tensor divergence, and vector Laplacians use the same finite-indexed\nconstruction. In particular,\n,\n,\n,\nand\n.\n\nFor each coordinate,  identifies\nevaluation of the reflected partial derivative with the corresponding\nanalytic derivative. The reflected gradient is the vector of these\ncoordinates, divergence is their finite sum, the Laplacian is the sum of the\ndiagonal second derivatives, and curl is the stated alternating combination.\nFunction extensionality and finite-sum congruence assemble the coordinate\nidentities into the four displayed operator identities.For the Laplacian, applying the first-derivative identity only at \nwould not suffice: two functions can agree at one point and have different\nderivatives there. Validity persists on a neighborhood of , so the\nanalytic first partial and the evaluated symbolic first partial agree\nthroughout that neighborhood. Their derivatives at  therefore agree.\nValidity of the first symbolic partial justifies the second application of\nthe derivative bridge. These are sufficient conditions on the intermediate\nformulas; the largest domain of smoothness can be bigger.\n\nIf the advected scalar expression is valid at the evaluation point,\nchecked symbolic advection evaluates directly to our analytic\nadvectiveDerivative. The componentwise convection and source-subtracted\ntransport residual theorems use the same bridge, so later proofs need not\ndepend on the reflected evaluator's coordinate-sum representation:\n.\n\nExpand symbolic advection as a finite coordinate sum and apply\n to each derivative term. The\nanalytic operator is ; expanding  in the Euclidean\nbasis identifies it with that sum.\n\nFor momentum component , assume ValidAt at  for  and .\nThe Navier–Stokes bridge also assumes validity of every first symbolic partial\nof ; the conservation bridge assumes validity of every flux component.\nThese give the stationary Euler and Navier–Stokes momentum residuals and the\ndivergence-form conservation residual. Incompressibility is a separate equation.\nThe undifferentiated viscosity and source terms need no validity hypotheses\nfor these identities. Componentwise, the Euler bridge gives\n,\nwhile the Navier–Stokes bridge adds  and the\nconservation bridge gives\n.\n\nNormalize each reflected residual, rewrite its differentiated components using\n, and identify the transport term\nwith . The remaining sums and products\nevaluate definitionally.\n\nThe viscosity term here is specifically . For a\nvariable coefficient this differs from divergence-form diffusion:\n\n\n\nThe extra term vanishes for constant viscosity. Keeping the exact operator\nvisible matters when applying a residual bridge: a certificate for one of\nthese expressions says nothing about the other.\n\nIn positive dimension, lower a differential equation against a closed-form\nansatz to a symbolic residual. If that residual evaluates to zero at the point\nand its analytic side conditions hold there, then\n.\n\nRewrite the analytic residual as the evaluation of the lowered residual,\nusing the analytic side conditions. ClosedResidualVanishesAt is exactly the\nassertion that this evaluation is zero, so it finishes the proof. A side-aware\nnormalizer is one way to supply that vanishing proof after its denominator\nand branch conditions have been discharged.\n\nThe integration entry point accepts a candidate  only after checking\nthat  normalizes to zero. Its certificate records\nrequirements for the primitive, the integrand, and the conditional\ncancellations used in that check.Fix a coordinate line . At each point where those\nrequirements hold, the evaluated primitive has derivative\n. If they hold for every\n, thenThe interval hypothesis is Set.uIcc a b in Lean. It includes both\nendpoints and every point between them, regardless of their order.\nValidity of the integrand supplies continuity and hence interval\nintegrability; validity of the primitive supplies the derivative needed by\nthe fundamental theorem of calculus. Every search method uses this same\ncertificate. At the reflected API, IntegrationCheck.certifyCandidate?\ntakes the coordinate, original integrand, and proposed primitive explicitly,\nso it can check a candidate without running primitive search. The ordinary\ninterval tactic uses this boundary for a supplied function or a common\nfunction reconstructed from the requested endpoint difference. It reflects\nthe integrand and candidate over one parameter table, so each coefficient\nhas the same meaning in both expressions. Checking a candidate against its\nown derivative would not establish the requested integral.\n\nFor substitutions, the proportionality checker validates its proposed factor with\n. Polynomial, rational,\nsubstitution, and integration-by-parts passes only propose candidates. The\nintegration checker validates the symbolic derivative identity with\n. Under the certificate's\nrequirements,  turns it into\nan analytic derivative along . The interval-wide requirements also\nmake the evaluated integrand continuous there, so the fundamental theorem\ngives the endpoint difference.\n\nThe differentiation rule tree in detail.\n\nDiffRule names the differentiation rules, and DiffDerivation records\ntheir application to an expression. For an expression e and variable index\ni, this tree connects the raw derivative e.diff i to the analytic partial\nderivative. Its rules are:\n\n* Constant rule. Every rational literal, , and  differentiates to zero: .* Variable rule. A coordinate  differentiates to the Kronecker delta: .* Sum, difference, and negation. These distribute linearly: .* Product rule. For : the derivative is .* Quotient rule. For  with  at the evaluation point: the derivative is .* Natural power rule. For : the derivative is .* Real power rule. For  with  at the evaluation point: the derivative is , the two terms of Mathlib's HasDerivAt.rpow; a literal natural exponent is lowered to the natural power rule instead.* Transcendental chain rules. Each elementary function composes with the inner derivative :* ,* ,* ,*  and  (requiring ),* ,*  (requiring  under Mathlib's total real logarithm),*  (requiring ).\n\nThe raw derivative is simplified by evaluating constant arithmetic,\neliminating multiplication by zero or one, and removing addition of zero.\nThese transformations preserve its value.\nA theorem may request a form that this simplifier does not reach, such as a\nfactored heat-kernel derivative or a square-root expression needing\n. The derivative tactic then tries the side-conditioned\nnormalizer, whose real-power and square-root rules identify the two forms\nunder the recorded conditions.\n\nExample: differentiating .\n\nConsider the expression  with variables  and\n in . Differentiating with respect to :\n\n1. The outermost constructor is sin, so the sine chain rule applies.2. The inner expression is , which uses the product rule.3. Differentiating  gives 1 (variable rule, Kronecker delta).4. Differentiating  with respect to  gives 0 (variable rule).5. The product rule yields .6. The chain rule produces .7. The simplifier reduces this to .\n\nThe bridge theorem then proves  at every point , as an identity in Lean's real analysis library.\n\nSupported antiderivative patterns.\n\nThe bounded antiderivative search IntegrationPlanner.candidateWithFuel?\ncombines seven mechanisms.\n\nPolynomial integration. Sparse multivariate polynomials are integrated\nterm by term. Each monomial  integrates in coordinate  to . The sparse representation avoids allocating zero coefficients.\n\nCalibrated substitution. For an integrand , the planner collects a list of substitution primitives. These include  for quotients by , inverse-power primitives, , , and . For each primitive, the proportionality checker (Alg.proportionality?) verifies whether the integrand equals the primitive's derivative times a factor independent of the integration coordinate. If successful, the candidate is that constant factor times the primitive.\n\nIntegration by parts. The LIATE heuristic ranks factors by their\nsuitability for differentiation. Logarithmic and inverse-trigonometric factors\nreceive rank 5, algebraic polynomial factors rank 4, trigonometric factors rank 3, and exponential factors rank 2.\nFor a product , the planner recursively integrates , forms , recursively integrates the correction, and validates the result. The initial recursion fuel is 96; this bounds recursive depth, not the total number of calls across search branches.\n\nExponential-trigonometric recurrences. For integrands of the form , the planner solves a finite coupled polynomial recurrence. Writing the antiderivative as , the amplitudes satisfy\n\n\n\nWhen , the constant matrix is invertible, and repeated matrix inversion lowers the polynomial degree. The process terminates in at most  steps.\n\nThe implemented route allows multivariate rational-polynomial amplitudes,\nwith the other coordinates treated as parameters, and common exponential and\ntrigonometric phases whose slopes in the selected coordinate are rational\nconstants.\n\nTrigonometric powers. The planner proposes primitives for sine and cosine\npowers with every fixed natural exponent. For positive powers, the phase\nderivative must simplify to an expression independent of the selected\ncoordinate and different from literal zero. The final certificate records the\nnonvanishing conditions needed for division. For constant nonzero slope\n, the low-power cases include:\n\n* ,* ,* ,* .\n\nRational function integration. For univariate rational functions , the pipeline proceeds in three stages:\n\n* Polynomial division. Exact sparse Euclidean division separates the improper part:  with . The polynomial quotient  is integrated term by term.* Hermite reduction. The proper fraction  undergoes Yun square-free decomposition of the denominator. For each repeated factor , the Hermite recurrence lowers the multiplicity by one at each step, producing rational correction terms that do not require logarithms.* Elementary transcendental integration. The terminal square-free fraction\nuses partial-fraction decomposition over the rationals. Linear factors give\nlogarithms. For a quadratic denominator , separate off the\nlogarithmic-derivative part. The remaining primitive uses arctangent when\n and real logarithms when .\n\nThe rational factor search combines rational-root extraction and exact\nKronecker search over Rat; extended Euclid supplies the modular inverses.\nIrrational scales in quadratic primitives remain exact symbolic square roots.\n\nBronstein (2005, Chapter 2) gives the rational-integration methods behind these\nstages. Here they are used in a bounded candidate search, and the accepted\nprimitive's derivative is checked.\n\nCompositional linearity. Sums, differences, negations, and scalar-independent multiples are handled compositionally.\n\nBefore a certificate's nonzero conditions reach a proof, a checked compactor\ncollects square-root products, extracts common factors, splits products and\npowers, normalizes factors, and deduplicates them. Its soundness theorem\nrecovers every raw obligation from the compact list.\n\nExample: integrating a rational function.\n\nConsider . The algebra to be checked is\n\n\n\nThus, on any interval avoiding  and , a candidate primitive is\n\n\n\nDifferentiating the middle term recovers the repeated pole; differentiating\nthe two logarithms recovers the simple poles. This concrete decomposition\nexplains the roles of the pipeline's stages:\n\n1. Polynomial division. The numerator has lower degree, so the quotient is zero.2. Square-free decomposition. The denominator factors as . Yun decomposition identifies  with multiplicity 2 and  with multiplicity 1.3. Hermite reduction. The recurrence absorbs the repeated factor, producing a rational correction and lowering the denominator to square-free form .4. Partial fractions. Modular inverse computation splits the residual into terms with denominators  and .5. Elementary integration. Each partial fraction  integrates to .6. Validation. The complete candidate is differentiated symbolically, and the residual is normalized to zero by the checked route. The requirement set records  and .\n\nTo use the endpoint formula, the entire closed interval must avoid both\npoles. Checking only  and  is insufficient: the\nendpoints ,  pass those tests but enclose the pole at .\nThis is why the integral theorem asks for nonvanishing at every point of\nSet.uIcc a b, while a pointwise derivative theorem asks only at its\nevaluation point.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Exact Symbolic Calculus","header":"10.6.2. Symbolic Calculus and Analytic Bridges","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus--Symbolic-Calculus-and-Analytic-Bridges"}});